---
/**
 * The one page on this site that computes rather than asserts.
 *
 * The ranking it prints is right for one bracket in three, which is exactly why it may
 * not be printed as a table of constants. Every figure here is `~/lib/placement`
 * arithmetic over `~/content/placement` data, run at whatever rates the reader gives it.
 *
 * The worked example below is rendered on the server from the same functions the island
 * runs, so a reader with scripting switched off still gets the finding and the queue.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import PlacementTool from "~/components/islands/PlacementTool";
import { fill, forfeitedCreditBp, percentOfBp, queue, ratePercent, shelf } from "~/components/islands/placement-model";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  defaultTaxRegime,
  placementAsOf,
  priorityRule,
  taxRegimes,
} from "~/content/placement";
import { citationHref } from "~/lib/citation";
import { derivedRates } from "~/lib/placement";

/** Equal nominal thirds in a Roth, a traditional account and a taxable one. */
const SHELTER_SHARE = 0.6667;

const opening = queue(shelf("paid-out"), defaultTaxRegime);
const placed = fill(opening, SHELTER_SHARE);
const forfeited = forfeitedCreditBp(placed);
const rates = derivedRates(defaultTaxRegime);

const foreign = opening.filter((row) => row.international);
const domestic = opening.filter((row) => row.international === false);
const weakestForeign = foreign[foreign.length - 1];
const strongestDomestic = domestic[0];
const last = opening[opening.length - 1];

const brackets = taxRegimes.length;
const source = citationHref(priorityRule.source);

const WHERE = { shelter: "Sheltered", split: "Partly sheltered", taxable: "Taxable" } as const;

/**
 * Written once, read twice: into `<thead>`, and into the label each cell shows when
 * the table becomes a list of cards below 40rem.
 */
const COLUMNS = ["#", "Fund", "Weight", "Saved per sheltered dollar", "Goes in"] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Where to hold each fund"
  description="Enter your tax bracket and roughly how your money splits across a Roth, a 401(k) and a taxable account. It ranks every fund we priced by what a sheltered dollar of it actually saves, and the answer is not the one you have read elsewhere."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Where to hold each fund</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        My shelter is smaller than my portfolio. Which funds go inside it? Checked against
        <code>docs/research/</code> on <span data-numeric>{placementAsOf}</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Most of the advice you will find says to shelter whatever carries the heaviest tax bill. That is right for
    bonds by a factor of four, and it is wrong for foreign funds in a way that flips the answer over.</p>

    <p>{priorityRule.plain}</p>

    <p><code>{priorityRule.formula}</code></p>

    <p>{priorityRule.whyForeignIsDifferent}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2>Run it at your own rates</h2>

    <p>Nothing you type goes anywhere. The page does the arithmetic in your browser and forgets it when you
    close the tab. The button underneath writes your setup into the address bar, if you want to send it to
    someone.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout class="mt-6">
    <PlacementTool client:visible />
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-12">
    <h2>Every foreign fund outranks every US fund</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Section 901">
      <p>
        A foreign tax credit offsets a US tax. A sheltered account owes none, so the withholding is simply gone,
        in a traditional account and a Roth alike.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>You have probably read the opposite: hold international in the taxable account so you can claim the
    foreign tax credit back. Sheltering all of them here does destroy <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(forfeited)}</span>
    a year of credit, permanently. It buys back several times that, because foreign funds yield
    more and a bigger dividend in a taxable account is a bigger tax bill.</p>

    <p>At <span data-numeric>{ratePercent(rates.qualifiedDividend)}%</span> on qualified dividends, the weakest
    foreign line on the fund list is {weakestForeign?.ticker} at
    <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(weakestForeign?.priorityBp ?? 0)}</span> saved per sheltered dollar, against
    {strongestDomestic?.ticker} at <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(strongestDomestic?.priorityBp ?? 0)}</span> for
    the strongest US line. The gap holds at every one of the {brackets} brackets the tool offers except the 0%
    one, where there is no credit to lose and nothing much to save either.</p>

    <p>And {last?.ticker} comes last, at <span data-numeric>{percentOfBp(last?.priorityBp ?? 0)}</span>. It is
    the cheapest, broadest, calmest fund we priced, which is exactly why it belongs in the account with no
    protection. Its dividend is the smallest tax bill you can put on a scarce sheltered dollar.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2>The queue at equal thirds</h2>

    <p>One investor, equal nominal thirds in a Roth, a traditional account and a taxable brokerage account, at
    the top marginal rates. Rendered here whether or not the tool above loaded.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-6" label="The fund list, ranked by what a sheltered dollar saves" maxWidth="42rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[36rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        The fund list ranked by what a sheltered dollar of each fund saves a year, at the top marginal rates, with
        two thirds of the portfolio sheltered
      </caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {
            COLUMNS.map((heading, i) => (
              <th
                role="columnheader"
                scope="col"
                class:list={["eyebrow py-2", i === 0 ? "pr-3 text-right" : "pr-4", (i === 2 || i === 3) && "text-right"]}
              >
                {heading}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          placed.map((row, index) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[0]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3 text-right text-ink-faint">
                {index + 1}
              </td>
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal text-ink">
                {row.ticker}
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[2]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {ratePercent(row.weight, 1)}%
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[3]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-semibold text-ink">
                {percentOfBp(row.priorityBp)}
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[4]} class="py-2 pr-4">
                {WHERE[row.where]}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-12">
    <h2>Three things this rests on</h2>

    <p><strong>The share of each dividend that gets the low rate.</strong> Part of what a fund pays out is taxed
    at the dividend rate and the rest at your income rate, which is 17 points dearer at the top bracket. Every
    figure above uses the fraction each sponsor filed rather than a round assumption: <span data-numeric
    >66.27%</span> for VEA, <span data-numeric>44.48%</span> for AVES, <span data-numeric>34.82%</span> for IEMG,
    <span data-numeric>25%</span> for IDMO. Assume they were all fully qualified instead and the answer for
    emerging markets flips over. That one input decides the sign.</p>

    <p><strong>The stacked fund files two readings of itself.</strong> It books income inside itself that it has
    not paid out. Count only what shareholders were taxed on and it sits seventh in the queue; count everything
    and it goes straight to the top, which is what the portfolio page prints. The tool defaults to the audited
    reading and offers the other. Either way the fund belongs in a shelter, and the asymmetry is about ten to
    one, so the measurement can stay open without stalling the decision.</p>

    <p><strong>Your yields are not these yields.</strong> Every figure is US federal, for one stated investor,
    on filings as of <span data-numeric>{placementAsOf}</span>. State income tax is left out and adds to every
    line. A yield is the input a ranking like this is most sensitive to, and none of these is point-in-time.</p>

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>Nothing here is personalised advice. Every figure is a function of stated inputs, and a different
      investor should restate them.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p class="text-sm">
      <a class="link" href={source} rel="noopener noreferrer">Structural and tax-aware edges</a>, checked
      <span data-numeric>{placementAsOf}</span>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
